\fs96 \cf2 Mojo tips for Python devs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo is designed with Python programmers in mind, but it isn't "just Python, only faster." Mojo introduces a type system, ownership-aware semantics, and low-level control that, as a Python developer, you may not have had to reason about to make your code work.\
This guide offers a practical resource for Python developers. It shows how familiar Python patterns translate into Mojo, where your instincts still apply and where Mojo asks you to build a new mental model.\
This isn't a tutorial. It's a core set of language migration tips and patterns to support you as you migrate to Mojo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Mojo's core model for Python developers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo looks like Python but its execution model is closer to Rust, Swift, C++, and other systems languages. Key differences from Python include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo is statically typed. In Python, types are optional hints that the interpreter mostly ignores at runtime. In Mojo, types are first-class. The compiler uses them to generate fast, specialized machine code.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo compiles to machine code. Python runs through an interpreter that translates your code at runtime, adding overhead to every operation. Mojo compiles directly to native machine code. This gives you fast and predictable performance, with no interpreter overhead.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo prefers value semantics and explicit mutability. In Python, most objects are mutable references, so assigning a list doesn't copy it. In Mojo, assigning or passing a value typically creates an independent copy. Changes to one value don't affect others unless you make sharing explicit. This keeps data flow clear and enables safe parallelism.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo supports modern ownership, but it doesn't trap you in "safe-only" abstractions. With ownership, the compiler tracks which variables and fields control a value's lifetime. That lets Mojo manage memory effectively, without a garbage collector or reference counting. When you need low-level control, such as interfacing with C-language libraries, you can manage memory explicitly using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo brings together ideas from Rust, C++, and Python. It combines Python's readability with a performance model inspired by systems languages like Rust and C++.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Understanding these differences is essential for writing correct, fast Mojo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Moving from Python to Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This section introduces a curated set of migration topics that explore common Mojo patterns.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Value semantics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Mojo, when you assign a value to a new variable, it's given a unique owned value, not a second reference pointing to the same data. This can catch new adopters off guard.\
In Python, both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 b
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  refer to the same list:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 b \cf5 =\cf13  a\cb1 \
\cb14 b\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf15 4\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 a\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # [1, 2, 3, 4]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # a changed because b and a both point to the same list\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In Mojo, assignment gives you a copy. If you're not working with trivial types (like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) or types with built-in copy semantics (like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), you may need to use an explicit copy call. You can also use and create types that are implicitly copyable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf18 "hello"\cf13   \cf6 # hello\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  b \cf5 =\cf13  a        \cf6 # hello, implicit copy\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 b \cf5 =\cf13  b \cf5 +\cf13  \cf18 " world"\cf13  \cf6 # hello world\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 a\cf5 )\cf13          \cf6 # hello\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 b\cf5 )\cf13          \cf6 # hello world\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  c\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  d \cf5 =\cf13  c\cf5 .\cf13 copy\cf5 ()\cf13  \cf6 # d is an independent copy\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 d\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf15 4\cf5 )\cf13       \cf6 # [1, 2, 3, 4]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 c\cf5 )\cf13          \cf6 # [1, 2, 3]  c is unchanged\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo uses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to declare variables. A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  binding owns its value. Using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  consistently makes your code easier to read. It's clear when you introduce a new binding and when you reassign an existing one.\
To use Python-like reference behavior, declare 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 b
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instead of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  a\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 ref\cf13  b \cf5 =\cf13  a \cf6 # b is a reference to the same value\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 b\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf15 4\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # The list updates. a still owns the list.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 a\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # [1, 2, 3, 4]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Mutability\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In both Python and Mojo, almost everything you create can be changed after the fact. In Mojo, there are some special rules.\
Python\
Nearly everything is mutable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 10\cf13        \cf6 # 10\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 b \cf5 =\cf13  a        \cf6 # 10\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 b \cf5 =\cf13  b \cf5 +\cf13  \cf15 10\cf13    \cf6 # 20\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 a\cf5 ,\cf13  b\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # 10 20\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo\
All variables are mutable by default. Function arguments aren't mutable by default.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  x \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 10\cf13  \cf6 # 10\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 x \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 20\cf13      \cf6 # 20, with a warning that x's previous assignment\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf6 # to 10 was never used\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 foo\cf5 (\cf13 value\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     value \cf5 +=\cf13  \cf15 1\cf13  \cf6 # Error, expression must be mutable\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  y \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 20\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 foo\cf5 (\cf13 y\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Default immutability in the function gives the compiler more room to optimize. Using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword in the argument declaration makes it mutable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 foo\cf5 (mut\cf13  value\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     value \cf5 +=\cf13  \cf15 1\cf13  \cf6 # This works\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Explicit mutability makes code easier to reason about because mutability is visible. You can look at a function signature and immediately see which values can change and which can't.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Numbers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Python gives you 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 float
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with arbitrary precision. Mojo takes a different approach: it uses explicit, fixed-width numeric types so the compiler can optimize aggressively and scale across parallel execution.\
Mojo provides concrete numeric types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int16
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float16
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Fixed width isn't a limitation in Mojo, it's a feature that lets the compiler pack numbers with known sizes into memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 SIMD types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In many languages, SIMD shows up later as a specialized tool for advanced users. In Mojo, SIMD is part of the core compute model. Mojo implements its primitive numeric types as SIMD values under the hood.\
That lets the compiler operate on multiple values with a single hardware instruction. When you choose the right numeric type, you give the compiler more room to generate faster code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Int types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Python\
Python's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  uses arbitrary precision. It can hold integers as large as memory allows.\
Mojo's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type\
The general 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type maps to your machine's native word size. This is typically 64 bits on a 64-bit system. You can always check:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 sys \cf5 import\cf13  size_of\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  a\cf5 :\cf13  Int \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 5\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  \cf19 bytes\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  size_of\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf19 bytes\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # 8 on a 64-bit system\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Floating point types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Python\
In Python, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 float
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is always 64-bit.\
Mojo\
Mojo floating point types aren't arbitrary precision. Mojo doesn't provide a default floating point type, the way it does with integers. That means there's no built-in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type.\
When you're just starting with Mojo, stick to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  floating point.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Division and types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo division behaves differently than in Python. In Python, dividing two integers always produces a float:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 7\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 b \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 2\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf15 7\cf13  \cf5 /\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 )\cf13    \cf6 # 3.5 \'97 always float\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In Mojo, if you want integer division to return a floating-point result, you must use explicit casting:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  a\cf5 :\cf13  Int \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 7\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  b\cf5 :\cf13  Int \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 2\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 Float64\cf5 (\cf13 a\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 /\cf13  Float64\cf5 (\cf13 b\cf5 ))\cf13      \cf6 # 3.5 \'97 explicit float division\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Mojo division operators\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Mojo, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 /
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  returns a value that always matches the type of the operands. A floating point number divided by a floating point number returns a floating point number, and an integer divided by an integer returns an integer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  a\cf5 :\cf13  Int \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 7\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  b\cf5 :\cf13  Int \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 2\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 a \cf5 /\cf13  b\cf5 )\cf13    \cf6 # 3, result type matches operand type\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Python programmers may be a bit surprised that / isn't "true division." It returns a truncated result, but the result is biased towards zero:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  c \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 -\cf15 7\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  d \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 2\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 c \cf5 /\cf13  d\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # -3, not -4, truncates towards zero\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 //
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  performs floored division, in the direction of negative infinity:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 c \cf5 //\cf13  d\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # -4, not -3, truncates towards negative infinity\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 /
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the type returned by 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 //
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is preserved from the operands:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13  Float64 \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 7.0\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  f\cf5 :\cf13  Float64 \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 2.0\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 e \cf5 //\cf13  f\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # 3.0, floors toward negative infinity\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 e \cf5 /\cf13  f\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # 3.5\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  g\cf5 :\cf13  Float64 \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 -\cf15 7.0\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 g \cf5 //\cf13  f\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # -4.0, floors toward negative infinity\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 g \cf5 /\cf13  f\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # -3.5\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Data structures: lists\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Python\
In Python, lists are dynamic. They can hold mixed types and grow freely.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 nums \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf18 "one"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2.0\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 nums\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf15 4\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # ['one', 2.0, 3, 4]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo\
A typed list holds only one element type. In this example, that type is Int. This allows the list implementation to pack data efficiently, using less space and improving performance. Mojo uses packed data rather than indirect references:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  nums\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 nums\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf15 4\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # [1, 2, 3, 4]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To store different kinds of values, define the element type as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that enumerates permitted types. Variant lets a single element type represent multiple concrete value types:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 utils \cf5 import\cf13  Variant\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  MixedType \cf5 =\cf13  Variant\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ,\cf13  Float64\cf5 ,\cf13  String\cf5 ,\cf13  Bool\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  mixed_list \cf5 =\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 MixedType\cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 mixed_list\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf13 MixedType\cf5 (\cf15 42\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 mixed_list\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf13 MixedType\cf5 (\cf15 3.14\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 mixed_list\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf13 MixedType\cf5 (\cf18 "hello"\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 mixed_list\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf13 MixedType\cf5 (\cf15 True\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  item \cf5 in\cf13  mixed_list\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 item\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # Output lines: 42, 3.14, hello, and True\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  tells the Mojo compiler which types are used, so it can allocate and manage memory correctly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Data structures: dictionaries\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Python dicts are dynamic. Keys and values can be anything. Mojo uses efficient dictionary implementations (Swiss tables) for fast data storage and retrieval. Typed dictionaries support efficient packing and data access.\
Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 counts \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 \{\cf18 "a"\cf5 :\cf13  \cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf18 "b"\cf5 :\cf13  \cf18 "two"\cf5 \}\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 counts\cf5 [\cf18 "c"\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 3.0\cf13  \cf6 # \{'a': 1, 'b': 'two', 'c': 3.0\}\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo\
A typed declaration like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict[String, Int]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  tells the compiler exactly what element types to expect for keys and values. This enables tighter, faster code. As with other Mojo collections, you can use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to broaden the range of permitted element types:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  counts\cf5 :\cf13  Dict\cf5 [\cf13 String\cf5 ,\cf13  Int\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 \{\cf18 "a"\cf5 :\cf13  \cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf18 "b"\cf5 :\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 \}\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 counts\cf5 [\cf18 "c"\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 3\cf13  \cf6 # \{a: 1, b: 2, c: 3\}\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Comprehensions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Python's comprehensions have direct Mojo analogs. The syntax is essentially identical.\
Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  list_squares \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf13 x \cf5 *\cf13  x \cf5 for\cf13  x \cf5 in\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 0\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 4\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 if\cf13  x \cf5 %\cf13  \cf15 2\cf13  \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf15 0\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # [0, 4, 16], list\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  positive_numbers \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf13 x \cf5 for\cf13  x \cf5 in\cf13  \cf19 range\cf5 (-\cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 if\cf13  x \cf5 >\cf13  \cf15 0\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # [1, 2], list\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  dict_squares \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 \{\cf13 x\cf5 :\cf13  x \cf5 *\cf13  x \cf5 for\cf13  x \cf5 in\cf13  \cf19 range\cf5 (\cf15 3\cf5 )\}\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 #  \{0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4\}, dict\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  upper_case \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 \{\cf13 k\cf5 :\cf13  v\cf5 .\cf13 upper\cf5 ()\cf13  \cf5 for\cf13  k\cf5 ,\cf13  v \cf5 in\cf13  \cf5 [(\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf18 "one"\cf5 ),\cf13  \cf5 (\cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf18 "two"\cf5 )]\}\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # \{1: ONE, 2: TWO\}, dict\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  number_set \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 \{\cf13 x \cf5 for\cf13  x \cf5 in\cf13  \cf19 range\cf5 (\cf15 5\cf5 )\}\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\}, set\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Iteration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In terms of syntax, Mojo's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 while
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loops align with Python. Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 break
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 continue
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for control flow.\
Mojo using a typed for-loop\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  nums \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 0\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 4\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  squares2\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 []\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  x \cf5 in\cf13  nums\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  x \cf5 %\cf13  \cf15 2\cf13  \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf15 0\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         squares2\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf13 x \cf5 *\cf13  x\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 squares2\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # [0, 4, 16]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo using a while loop\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  squares3\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 []\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  idx \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 0\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 while\cf13  idx \cf5 <\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     squares3\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf13 idx \cf5 *\cf13  idx\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     idx \cf5 +=\cf13  \cf15 1\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 squares3\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # [0, 1, 4]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Function definitions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Python, you can write a function without specifying the types of its arguments or return value. In Mojo, you must declare types explicitly.\
Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 add\cf5 (\cf13 a\cf5 ,\cf13  b\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  a \cf5 +\cf13  b\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 add\cf5 (\cf13 a\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ,\cf13  b\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  a \cf5 +\cf13  b\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The optional 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ->
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax declares the return type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Error handling\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Error handling in Mojo looks very similar to Python. You raise and catch exceptions.\
Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  ValueError\cf5 (\cf18 "bad input"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  ValueError \cf5 as\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 e\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # bad input\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword in function and method declarations indicates that a function may generate or propagate errors.\
Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  Error\cf5 (\cf18 "bad input"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 e\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # bad input\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can specify error types by adding a type name after the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword. This lets you catch the error and use the type instance directly in your 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf13  \cf19 MyCustomError\cf5 (\cf13 Writable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  message\cf5 :\cf13  String\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 test_typed_error\cf5 ()\cf13  raises MyCustomError\cf5 :\cf13  \cf6 # Typed error\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  MyCustomError\cf5 (\cf18 "custom error occurred"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     test_typed_error\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 e\cf5 .\cf13 message\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # custom error occurred\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Functions that don't handle the errors they raise automatically delegate error handling to their caller. You must declare these functions with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 another_raising_function\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  Error\cf5 (\cf18 "Message"\cf5 )\cf13      \cf6 # Error raised here\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 raising_function\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     another_raising_function\cf5 ()\cf13  \cf6 # Error continues to pass\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 handles_errors\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         raising_function\cf5 ()\cf13      \cf6 # Error handled in this non-raising function\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # handle error here\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that in Mojo, each 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try/except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement can handle a single error type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Types: classes vs structs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Python classes are flexible and dynamic. You can add attributes at runtime, mix types, and override behavior freely. Mojo uses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 struct
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , a statically typed alternative that the compiler can optimize aggressively.\
Mojo structs are stack-allocated. The value lives in a fast, fixed-size region of memory rather than on the heap, where a garbage collector must track and clean it up.\
Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 class\cf13  \cf19 Point\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf19 __init__\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  x\cf5 ,\cf13  y\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf13 x \cf5 =\cf13  x\cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf13 y \cf5 =\cf13  y\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo\
Mojo initializers require 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword indicates that the method returns a value through an argument. In initializers, that argument is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and the instance's fields are guaranteed to be fully initialized:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 struct\cf13  \cf19 Point\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  x\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  y\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf19 __init__\cf5 (out\cf13  self\cf5 ,\cf13  x\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ,\cf13  y\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf13 x \cf5 =\cf13  x\cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf13 y \cf5 =\cf13  y\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf19 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  point \cf5 =\cf13  Point\cf5 (\cf15 5\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 point\cf5 .\cf13 x\cf5 ,\cf13  point\cf5 .\cf13 y\cf5 )\cf13  \cf6 # 5, 3\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Structs deliver performance and predictability.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Types: variable static typing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Python\
In Python, you may assign different types to the same variable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf18 "x"\cf13  \cf6 # String\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 10\cf13   \cf6 # Not an error\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo\
In Mojo, once a name is bound in a scope, its type is fixed and can't be rebound to a different type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 1\cf13     \cf6 # Int\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf18 "string"\cf13  \cf6 # Error: can't implicitly convert String to Int\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When you use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can switch between the types it enumerates. The variable itself remains statically typed as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , even though the concrete value it holds may change:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 utils \cf5 import\cf13  Variant\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 testing \cf5 import\cf13  \cf5 *\cf13 \cb1 \
\
